const AGENT_URL="";
const BR=[
["Fundamentals","#1a6b45",["Atoms|Everything is made of atoms: a tiny nucleus (protons and neutrons) with electrons buzzing around it. The number of protons decides which element it is.","Chemical bonding|Atoms swap or share electrons to become stable. Swap = ionic (table salt), share = covalent (water), a pooled “sea” of electrons = metallic.","Periodic trends|The table is ordered by proton count. Across a row atoms hold electrons tighter; down a column they get bigger and let go of electrons more easily.","States of matter|Solid, liquid and gas depend on how fast particles move versus how strongly they attract. Heating adds motion, cooling removes it.","Moles and stoichiometry|A mole is 6.022×10²³ particles, a chemist’s “dozen”. A balanced equation is a recipe telling how many moles react.","Solutions|Dissolving spreads one substance through another. Molarity = moles of solute per litre. Like dissolves like: polar in polar, oily in oily.","Acids and bases|Acids donate H⁺, bases accept it. pH 7 is neutral, lower is acidic, higher is basic, and each step is a tenfold change.","Equilibrium|Reversible reactions settle when forward and backward speeds match. Le Chatelier: push on the system and it pushes back.","Redox|Oxidation loses electrons, reduction gains them (OIL RIG). Rusting, burning and batteries are all electron transfers."]],
["Physical","#1d5fa8",["Thermodynamics|Tracks energy and disorder. A reaction tends to go when it lowers free energy: ΔG = ΔH − TΔS.","Kinetics|How fast reactions happen. Molecules need enough energy to collide productively (activation energy); a catalyst is a lower mountain pass.","Quantum chemistry|Electrons behave like waves and live in orbitals, clouds of probability, not neat orbits. Schrödinger’s equation gives their allowed energies.","Spectroscopy|Shine light or radio waves on matter and see what is absorbed. Every molecule leaves a unique fingerprint (IR, UV-Vis, NMR).","Electrochemistry|Links electricity and reactions: batteries turn chemistry into current, electrolysis uses current to force reactions.","Photochemistry|Reactions started by light: photosynthesis, vision, sunscreen and photography.","Surface and colloid chemistry|Matters when things are tiny: catalysts work on surfaces, while milk, fog and paint are colloids.","Statistical mechanics|Explains big properties like temperature and pressure from the average behaviour of countless molecules."]],
["Organic","#6b3fa0",["Hydrocarbons|Chains and rings of only carbon and hydrogen: alkanes (single bonds), alkenes (double), alkynes (triple) and benzene rings. Most fuels.","Functional groups|Small atom groups that give molecules their personality: –OH alcohol, –COOH acid, –NH₂ amine, C=O carbonyl.","Stereochemistry|Same atoms, different 3D shape. Mirror-image molecules, like left and right hands, can act very differently in the body.","Reaction mechanisms|Step-by-step electron movement (curved arrows) showing how bonds break and form: SN1, SN2, E1, E2, additions.","Polymers|Long chains built from repeating units (monomers): polyethylene, nylon, proteins, DNA.","Medicinal chemistry|Designing molecules that fit biological targets like a key in a lock: antibiotics, painkillers, cancer drugs.","Organometallic chemistry|Carbon bonded to metals. Grignard reagents and many catalysts build complex molecules this way.","Natural products|Molecules made by plants, microbes and animals, such as caffeine, penicillin and taxol, that inspire new drugs."]],
["Inorganic","#c2561f",["Coordination chemistry|A metal ion surrounded by ligands. Gives colour and function: haemoglobin, chlorophyll, the cancer drug cisplatin.","Main-group chemistry|The s- and p-block elements: salts, oxides, halogens, and nitrogen, sulfur and phosphorus compounds.","Transition metals|d-block metals with many oxidation states and bright colours; excellent catalysts (Fe, Ni, Pt).","Crystal field theory|Explains why metal complexes are coloured: ligands split the d-orbitals and electrons jump between them.","Solid-state chemistry|How atoms pack in crystals. The base of semiconductors, superconductors and ceramics.","Bioinorganic chemistry|Metals in life: iron carries oxygen, zinc runs enzymes, calcium builds bones."]],
["Analytical","#0f7a8a",["Qualitative and quantitative|Qualitative asks “what is in it?”, quantitative asks “how much?”. Together they identify and measure.","Titration|Add a solution of known strength drop by drop until the reaction just finishes, then calculate the unknown amount.","Chromatography|Separates a mixture as it travels through a material; each part moves at its own speed (TLC, GC, HPLC).","Mass spectrometry|Weighs charged molecules to identify them and detect tiny traces.","NMR and IR analysis|Use magnetism and infrared light to map a molecule’s structure atom by atom.","Electroanalytical methods|Measure voltage or current to detect substances, as in pH meters and glucose sensors."]],
["Biochemistry","#c2306b",["Proteins|Chains of amino acids folded into 3D shapes that build, move and defend your body.","Enzymes|Protein catalysts that speed up reactions millions of times; the substrate fits like a key in a lock.","Carbohydrates|Sugars and starches: quick energy, plus structural fibres like cellulose.","Lipids|Fats, oils and membranes: store energy and wrap every cell.","Nucleic acids|DNA stores the instructions, RNA copies and reads them. Four letters (A, T/U, G, C) spell the code.","Metabolism|All reactions in a cell. Catabolism breaks food down to make ATP, anabolism builds molecules.","Molecular biology|How genes become proteins: replication, transcription, translation.","Clinical and pharmacology|How body chemistry changes in disease and how drugs act on it."]],
["Nuclear","#9a7b00",["Radioactive decay|Unstable nuclei release alpha, beta or gamma radiation to settle down. Half-life is the time for half of them to decay.","Fission and fusion|Splitting heavy nuclei (power plants) or joining light ones (the Sun) releases huge energy, E = mc².","Isotopes and dating|Same element, different neutrons. Carbon-14 decay dates ancient remains.","Radiopharmaceuticals|Tracers like technetium-99m let doctors image organs and treat some cancers."]],
["Environmental","#2c8a3a",["Atmospheric chemistry|Ozone, smog, acid rain and greenhouse gases are all reactions in the air.","Water chemistry|Hardness, pH, dissolved oxygen and the treatment steps that make water safe.","Green chemistry|Designing processes that use less energy, avoid toxic materials and create little waste.","Pollution and remediation|Tracking contaminants and cleaning soil and water using chemistry.","Climate chemistry|CO₂ traps heat; the ocean absorbing it also becomes slightly more acidic."]],
["Industrial","#55606e",["Haber–Bosch process|Turns nitrogen from air plus hydrogen into ammonia for fertiliser, helping feed billions.","Petrochemicals|Crude oil is split into fuels and building blocks for plastics, detergents and drugs.","Polymers and plastics|Industrial production of PET, PVC, Kevlar and silicones.","Nanomaterials|Particles of 1–100 nm behave differently: quantum dots, nanotubes, graphene.","Batteries and energy|Li-ion cells shuttle ions between electrodes; fuel cells combine hydrogen and oxygen for electricity.","Glass and ceramics|Heated mineral materials with strong, heat-resistant structures.","Corrosion|Metals reacting with oxygen and water, slowed by coatings, galvanising and alloys."]],
["Computational","#4a4ac2",["Molecular modelling|Computers simulate how molecules move and fit together.","Density functional theory|A fast quantum method that predicts energies and shapes from electron density.","Cheminformatics|Storing, searching and comparing millions of molecules with data tools.","Machine learning in chemistry|AI predicts properties, reactions and drug candidates; AlphaFold predicts protein shapes."]],
["Specialty","#b5651d",["Food chemistry|Browning (Maillard reaction), emulsions, preservatives and flavour molecules.","Forensic chemistry|Identifying drugs, fibres, residues and poisons from tiny traces of evidence.","Astrochemistry|Molecules in space clouds, stars and comets, where life’s ingredients may form.","Geochemistry|The chemical makeup of rocks, minerals, oceans and Earth’s history.","Agricultural chemistry|Fertilisers, pesticides and soil nutrients.","Consumer chemistry|Soaps, shampoo, perfume and sunscreen formulations.","Supramolecular chemistry|Molecules held together by weak forces that self-assemble into larger structures."]]];
