const DF=["Gross pay is what you earn before cuts. Tax and other deductions come off, leaving take-home pay. Income can come from a salary, hourly wages, freelance fees or business profit, and it grows with skills, experience and the value you create.|Gross pay of 50,000 with 8,000 in deductions leaves 42,000 to spend, save and invest.|Budget from take-home pay, not gross. Relying on one income source is risky, so build a second one slowly.", "A budget is a plan for every unit of income. List income, then fixed costs (rent, bills), variable costs (food, travel) and savings. Compare plan with actual spending each month and adjust. The 50/30/20 rule gives 50% to needs, 30% to wants and 20% to saving or debt.|With 40,000 income: 20,000 needs, 12,000 wants, 8,000 savings.|A budget that is too strict fails. Leave a small fun allowance and review monthly.", "Pay yourself first: move money to savings the day income arrives, using an automatic transfer. Keep short-term savings somewhere safe and easy to reach, such as a savings account, and give each pot a purpose.|Saving 5,000 a month builds 60,000 in a year with no extra effort once it is automatic.|Saving what is left after spending usually means saving nothing. Save first, spend the rest.", "This is money kept only for shocks: job loss, medical bills or urgent repairs. Aim for three to six months of essential expenses in a safe account you can reach within a day or two.|If essentials cost 25,000 a month, target 75,000 to 150,000.|Start with one month and grow it. Do not put it in risky assets or spend it on sales and holidays.", "A credit report records your loans and repayment history, and a score summarises it. Lenders use it to decide approval and interest rate. On-time payment matters most, then low card use compared with your limit, a long history and few new applications.|Using 20,000 of a 100,000 limit is 20% utilization, which looks healthy.|One missed payment can lower your score. Check your report yearly for errors.", "Interest is the price of borrowing. A loan has a principal, a rate and a term, and each repayment covers interest plus some principal. Useful debt builds value (education, a home); harmful debt funds things that lose value at high rates.|Avalanche: pay the highest-rate debt first to save the most interest. Snowball: clear the smallest first for motivation.|Read the total repayment, fees and penalties before signing, not just the monthly amount.", "A card is a short-term loan. If you pay the full statement balance by the due date you usually pay no interest. Otherwise interest starts, often at a very high yearly rate, and minimum payments keep you in debt for years.|Spending 10,000 and paying only the minimum can cost far more than 10,000 in total.|Reward points are not worth paying interest. Avoid cash withdrawals on cards, as fees and interest start immediately.", "Investing puts money into assets that can grow: stocks (part ownership of companies), bonds (loans to governments or firms), mutual and index funds (baskets of assets) and real estate. Higher possible returns come with higher risk, so match choices to your goal and time horizon.|An index fund buys many companies at once, so one failure matters little.|Do not invest money you need within a few years in stocks, and avoid anyone promising guaranteed high returns.", "Interest earns interest. Each period, returns are added to the balance and the bigger balance earns more next time. Growth looks slow at first, then speeds up, so time matters more than the amount.|1,000 at 8% a year becomes about 2,159 in 10 years and about 10,063 in 30 years with no extra deposits.|It works against you on debt too. Starting five years earlier often beats saving more later.", "Risk is the chance that results differ from what you expect, including losses. Diversification spreads money across asset types, industries and countries so one bad result does not sink everything. Asset allocation is how you split it.|Holding stocks, bonds and some gold means a stock fall may be softened by the other assets.|Diversification reduces risk but never removes it. Do not put everything into one stock, even a famous one.", "Work out the yearly income you will need, then how many years you have to build it. Regular contributions to tax-advantaged retirement accounts, such as provident funds or pension schemes, grow through compounding and employer contributions.|Saving 10% of salary from age 25 builds a far bigger fund than 20% from age 40.|Avoid early withdrawals, and remember inflation: costs will be higher when you retire.", "Insurance moves a large, unlikely loss to an insurer in exchange for a regular premium. Health insurance covers hospital bills, term life insurance supports your family if you die, and vehicle and home insurance cover damage and theft.|A hospital stay costing 500,000 is paid by the insurer, while you paid only a small yearly premium.|Buy insurance for protection, not as an investment. Read exclusions, waiting periods and claim rules first.", "Governments collect tax on income, spending and property to fund services. Income tax is usually progressive: higher slices are taxed at higher rates. Deductions and exemptions reduce taxable income, and you must file by the deadline.|If income is 600,000 and eligible deductions are 100,000, tax is calculated on 500,000.|Keep receipts and records, file on time to avoid penalties, and check current rules because they change.", "Inflation is the general rise in prices. If prices rise 6% a year, 100 buys only about 94 worth of goods next year. Savings earning less than inflation lose buying power, so long-term goals need growth assets.|A snack costing 20 today costs about 36 in ten years at 6% inflation.|Compare your return with inflation (the real return) and avoid leaving large sums idle in cash for years.", "Needs come before wants. Compare total cost (price, delivery, running and repair costs), check reviews and warranty, and wait before big purchases. Sellers use urgency, anchoring and bundles to push you to spend.|A cheap printer with costly ink can cost more than a pricier one over two years.|Be wary of limited-time pressure and buy-now-pay-later schemes that hide interest and fees.", "Scammers copy banks, officials and companies, create urgency, and ask you to act fast: share an OTP, install an app or pay a fee. Common types are phishing messages, fake investment schemes, lottery and job scams, and UPI or OTP fraud.|A caller says your account will be blocked and asks for your OTP. This is a scam, because banks never ask for it.|Never share passwords, PINs or OTPs. Stop, verify through the official number, and report fraud quickly.", "Turn wishes into goals with an amount, a deadline and a monthly plan. Short-term goals (under 1 year), medium-term (1 to 5 years) and long-term (5+ years) need different places for your money: safe options for near goals, growth assets for far ones.|To save 120,000 for a laptop and a course in 2 years, put away 5,000 each month.|Write goals down, rank them, and review once a year as life changes."],DC=["Programs are step-by-step instructions. You store data in variables, make decisions with if statements, repeat with loops and reuse code with functions. A language such as Python or JavaScript is translated so the computer can run it.|In Python, a loop that adds the numbers 1 to 10 takes three lines and gives 55.|Start with one language, build small projects, and read error messages: they usually point to the problem.", "A data structure organizes data (arrays, lists, stacks, queues, trees, graphs, hash tables). An algorithm is a procedure that solves a problem using it. Big-O notation describes how running time grows as input grows.|Binary search finds a name among 1,000,000 sorted items in about 20 steps, while checking one by one may take a million.|Choose the structure that fits the operations you need most, then think about time and memory.", "The CPU fetches, decodes and executes instructions, using registers and fast caches to avoid waiting for slower RAM. Storage keeps data permanently. GPUs have thousands of small cores for parallel work such as graphics and AI.|A cache is like keeping your notes on the desk instead of walking to the library each time.|More cores or GHz does not always mean faster: memory speed and software matter too.", "The operating system manages hardware for programs. It schedules processes on the CPU, gives each one memory, organizes files, and controls devices and security. The kernel is its core, and programs talk to it through system calls.|With many apps open, the scheduler switches between them so quickly that they seem to run together.|When RAM runs out the system uses slower disk space (swap), which makes it lag.", "Data travels as small packets across routers using TCP/IP. DNS turns names like example.com into IP addresses, then HTTP or HTTPS requests the page from a server. Wi-Fi is the wireless link to your router, which connects you to your ISP.|Typing a web address triggers a DNS lookup, a connection to the server and the page returning in packets.|Public Wi-Fi can be risky: prefer HTTPS sites and avoid sensitive logins.", "A database stores data so it can be searched and changed safely. Relational databases keep tables of rows and columns queried with SQL; NoSQL stores flexible documents or key-value data. Indexes speed up searches and transactions keep updates all-or-nothing.|A bank transfer subtracts from one account and adds to another in one transaction, or neither happens.|Design tables carefully, keep backups, and never build SQL by pasting user input, to avoid injection.", "The front end (HTML for structure, CSS for style, JavaScript for behaviour) runs in the browser. The back end runs on a server, handles logic and talks to a database. APIs let the two exchange data, usually as JSON.|When you press Like, JavaScript sends an API request, the server saves it and the page updates.|Make pages responsive for phones, accessible for everyone and quick to load.", "Real software is built by teams. Version control (Git) tracks changes, tests catch bugs early, code review improves quality, and CI/CD automatically tests and deploys. Agile means building in small steps with regular feedback.|A developer pushes code, tests run automatically, and if they pass the change goes live.|Write clear, simple code and commit often. Untested, unreviewed changes cause many outages.", "Machine learning finds patterns in data instead of using hand-written rules. A neural network adjusts millions of numbers (weights) to reduce its errors during training. Language models predict the next word at huge scale, and vision models learn from labelled images.|A spam filter learns from thousands of emails marked spam or not spam.|AI can be wrong or biased, so check important answers and know what data it learned from.", "Security protects the confidentiality, integrity and availability of data. Encryption scrambles data, authentication proves who you are, and layers such as firewalls, updates and least-privilege access limit damage. Common attacks are phishing, malware and weak passwords.|Two-factor authentication stops most attacks even when a password leaks.|Use a password manager, unique passwords, updates, and caution with links and attachments.", "Cloud computing rents servers, storage and services over the internet. Virtual machines share one physical computer, containers (like Docker) package an app with what it needs, and serverless runs code only when triggered. DevOps joins development and operations through automation.|A shop website can add servers automatically during a sale and remove them afterwards.|Watch costs and permissions: unused resources and public storage settings cause surprise bills and leaks.", "Data science turns raw data into decisions. The steps are collect, clean, explore, model and communicate. Statistics tells you whether a pattern is real, and charts help others understand it. Pipelines automate the flow of data.|Analysing sales by month may show demand peaking before festivals, so stock is planned earlier.|Bad data gives bad answers, and correlation is not causation.", "HCI studies how people use technology. Good design is easy to learn, efficient and forgiving. Designers observe users, sketch prototypes, test them and improve. Accessibility makes sure people with different abilities can use products too.|Large buttons, clear labels and an undo option make an app easier for everyone.|Design for real users, not for yourself, and test with a few people early.", "Quantum computers use qubits, which can be in a mix of 0 and 1 (superposition) and linked by entanglement. Algorithms are designed so wrong answers cancel out and right ones stand out. They suit specific problems such as simulating molecules or factoring numbers.|Shor's algorithm could factor huge numbers quickly, which is why new encryption is being developed.|They will not replace normal computers, and today's machines are small and error-prone.", "Robots sense (cameras, lidar, touch), think (a controller or AI) and act (motors). IoT connects everyday devices with sensors to the internet using small embedded computers such as Arduino or Raspberry Pi.|A smart irrigation system reads soil moisture and switches the pump on only when needed.|Connected devices need updates and strong passwords because they are easy targets.", "Computer graphics turns 3D models into pixels. Rendering projects shapes onto the screen, adds lighting and textures, and GPUs do the work in parallel. Game engines such as Unity and Unreal combine graphics, physics, sound and logic in a loop that runs about 60 times a second.|Each frame, the game reads input, updates the world and draws the scene again.|Performance is a budget: more detail means a lower frame rate.", "This field studies what computers can and cannot do. Automata model simple machines, Turing machines define what is computable, and complexity classes such as P and NP group problems by how hard they are to solve.|The halting problem proves that no program can always tell whether another program will finish.|P versus NP, whether checking an answer is as easy as finding it, is still unsolved."];
