F.push("Stock market basics|Buying small ownership slices of companies through exchanges, and how prices, indices and returns work.","Mutual funds and ETFs|Pooled baskets of investments run by professionals or tracking an index, with different costs and rules.","Bonds and fixed income|Lending to governments or companies in return for regular interest and your money back at maturity.","Real estate|Property as a home or an investment: rent, appreciation, loans, costs and illiquidity.","Banking and accounts|Savings, current and fixed deposits, interest, fees, and how deposits are protected.","Mortgages and home loans|Borrowing to buy property: down payment, EMI, rate types, tenure and total cost.","Net worth and balance sheet|Assets minus liabilities: the single number that shows where you really stand.","Cryptocurrency|Digital assets on blockchains: how they work, why they swing wildly, and the risks involved.","Digital payments and UPI|Paying by phone, cards and wallets safely, and how instant bank-to-bank transfers work.","Starting a business|Turning an idea into income: funding, costs, pricing, profit versus cash flow and legal basics.");
DF.push("A stock is a share of ownership in a company. Companies sell shares to raise money, and investors trade them on exchanges such as the NSE, BSE, NYSE or Nasdaq. Prices move with supply and demand, which respond to company profits, growth outlook, interest rates and news. You earn in two ways: the price rising (capital gain) and dividends, a share of profit paid out. An index such as the Nifty 50 or S&P 500 tracks a group of large companies and shows how the market as a whole is doing. Over many decades stocks have beaten most other assets, but with sharp falls along the way, including drops of 30 to 50% in crashes. Key terms: market capitalization (share price times number of shares), P/E ratio (price divided by yearly earnings per share), bull market (rising) and bear market (falling).|You buy 10 shares at 200 each, spending 2,000. A year later the price is 240 and the company paid 4 per share in dividends. Your gain is 400 from price plus 40 from dividends, a 22% return. If instead the price fell to 150 you would be down 500 on paper, and you only lose for real if you sell.|Do not buy because a stock is trending or a stranger gave a tip. Never invest money you need within about five years. Trying to time the market usually fails: missing just a handful of the best days can cut long-run returns sharply. Beginners are often better served by low-cost index funds than by picking single stocks.",
"A mutual fund collects money from many investors and a fund manager invests it in stocks, bonds or both. You own units, priced by net asset value (NAV) once a day. An ETF (exchange-traded fund) is similar but trades on an exchange all day like a share. Index funds simply copy an index, so they need little management and charge low fees. Active funds try to beat the market and charge more. The expense ratio is the yearly fee taken from the fund, and over decades even 1% extra can eat a large share of your gains. A SIP (systematic investment plan) invests a fixed sum every month, which spreads your buying across highs and lows (rupee-cost averaging).|Investing 5,000 a month through a SIP in an index fund at an assumed 10% yearly return grows to roughly 10 lakh in 10 years, from 6 lakh actually invested. Two funds with the same 10% gross return but fees of 0.2% and 1.5% differ by several lakhs over 20 years.|Past returns do not promise future ones. Check the expense ratio, the fund's holdings and the risk label, and avoid frequent switching. Do not stop a SIP in a market fall, because that is when units are cheapest.",
"A bond is a loan you give to a government or company. In return you receive regular interest (the coupon) and your principal back on the maturity date. Prices move opposite to interest rates: when new bonds pay more, older lower-paying bonds fall in value. Credit ratings (AAA down to D) estimate the chance the issuer fails to pay, and riskier issuers pay higher yields. Government bonds are usually the safest, corporate bonds pay more, and long maturities swing more in price than short ones. Bonds usually fall less than stocks in a crash, so they steady a portfolio.|You buy a 1,000 bond with a 7% coupon for 5 years. You receive 70 each year, then 1,000 back at the end, totalling 1,350. If market rates rise to 9% right after, you can still hold to maturity and receive every promised payment.|High yield can mean high default risk. Check the rating and the issuer, and do not assume a bond is risk free. Inflation can also reduce the real value of fixed payments.",
"Real estate is land and buildings. You can earn from rent and from the property rising in value over time. Costs include the purchase price, registration and stamp duty, maintenance, property tax, insurance and sometimes agent fees. Property is illiquid: selling can take months and costs money. Most buyers use a loan, which magnifies both gains and losses (leverage). A common check is the rental yield: yearly rent divided by the property price. Alternatives with less hassle are REITs, which let you own a share of rental properties like a stock.|A flat costing 60 lakh rents for 20,000 a month, a yearly yield of 4%. If you add 3 lakh yearly in loan interest, tax and upkeep, the net return may be lower than a fixed deposit unless the price also rises.|Do not treat a home you live in as automatically a great investment. Include every cost, keep an emergency fund after the down payment, and avoid stretching so far that the EMI crowds out everything else.",
"Banks keep your money safe and lend it out. A savings account pays modest interest and allows easy withdrawals. A current account suits businesses and pays little. A fixed deposit locks money for a set term at a higher rate, and a recurring deposit builds a habit of monthly saving. Interest is usually quoted yearly, and compounding frequency matters. Deposits are insured up to a limit by the national scheme (in India, DICGC covers up to 5 lakh per depositor per bank). Watch for fees: minimum balance charges, ATM limits and penalties for breaking a deposit early.|5,00,000 in a fixed deposit at 7% for 3 years grows to about 6,16,000 with quarterly compounding. The same money in a savings account at 3% grows to about 5,46,000.|Do not keep large sums idle in a zero-interest account, and do not put all savings above the insurance limit in one bank. Check the real return after inflation and tax.",
"A mortgage or home loan lets you buy property by paying a down payment (often 10 to 25%) and borrowing the rest. You repay in EMIs (equated monthly instalments) over 10 to 30 years. Early EMIs are mostly interest and later ones mostly principal. Rates can be fixed or floating (linked to a benchmark that changes). The EMI formula is EMI = P × r × (1+r)^n ÷ ((1+r)^n − 1), where r is the monthly rate and n the number of months. A longer tenure gives a smaller EMI but far more total interest. Prepaying part of the principal early saves a large amount.|A loan of 40 lakh at 9% for 20 years has an EMI of about 35,990, so you pay about 86 lakh in total, more than double the amount borrowed. At 15 years the EMI is about 40,570 but the total is about 73 lakh.|Keep total EMIs under roughly 40% of take-home pay. Read the fine print on processing fees, prepayment penalties and rate resets, and keep money for registration, furnishing and emergencies.",
"Net worth is everything you own (assets) minus everything you owe (liabilities). Assets include cash, bank balances, investments, property and vehicles. Liabilities include loans, card balances and other dues. A balance sheet lists both on one page. Tracking net worth every few months shows real progress better than income alone, because someone earning a lot but spending it all can have a low or negative net worth. Distinguish assets that grow or produce income from those that lose value, such as cars and gadgets.|Assets: 2 lakh bank, 5 lakh investments, 6 lakh car value = 13 lakh. Liabilities: 4 lakh car loan, 50,000 card dues = 4.5 lakh. Net worth = 8.5 lakh. Paying off the card dues instantly raises it by nothing on paper but saves the interest.|Do not count items at their purchase price. Use a realistic resale value, and review it regularly rather than obsessing over daily changes.",
"Cryptocurrencies such as Bitcoin and Ethereum are digital assets recorded on a blockchain, a shared ledger copied across many computers and secured by cryptography. There is no central bank behind them, and prices are set by trading demand. Bitcoin has a fixed supply of 21 million coins. Supporters see digital scarcity and open finance. Critics point to extreme volatility, scams, hacks, energy use in some systems and the lack of any cash flow behind most coins. Regulation and tax treatment differ by country and keep changing, so check the current rules where you live.|A coin bought at 100 can fall to 40 in weeks, a 60% loss, and has done so repeatedly. Someone who put in only 2% of their savings could absorb that, while someone who put in everything could not.|Never invest more than you can afford to lose entirely. Beware guaranteed returns, celebrity promotions and apps asking for your seed phrase. Lost keys or a wrong address mean money is gone with no bank to reverse it.",
"Digital payments move money without cash. UPI links your bank account to a phone app and a virtual payment address, so a transfer completes in seconds after you approve it with your PIN. Cards use networks such as Visa and RuPay, wallets store prepaid money, and net banking works through your bank's website. Each method has different fees, limits and protections. Fraud usually targets people, not the technology, by tricking them into approving a payment or sharing codes.|Scanning a shop's QR code, entering the amount and your UPI PIN sends money instantly. Money is only received when you do not enter a PIN: if someone says you will get money and asks for your PIN, it is a scam.|You never enter a PIN to receive money. Do not share OTPs, screen-share with strangers, or click payment links from unknown senders. Report fraud to your bank and the cyber crime helpline quickly.",
"A business earns money by selling something customers value for more than it costs to provide. Start by testing the idea cheaply with a few customers. Know your fixed costs (rent, salaries), variable costs (materials per sale), price and break-even point, the number of sales needed to cover costs. Profit is revenue minus costs, but cash flow is the timing of money in and out, and many profitable firms fail by running out of cash. Funding comes from savings, loans, investors or customers paying upfront. Keep business and personal money separate and choose a legal structure such as sole proprietorship, partnership or company.|A cafe sells coffee at 120 with 40 of ingredients, a margin of 80. With 60,000 monthly fixed costs, it must sell 750 cups a month to break even.|Do not mix personal and business accounts, ignore taxes and registration, or spend all the loan before first sales. Keep several months of running costs in reserve.");
