/* ===== v10: toys, plain-words articles, orbit look ===== */
PLAIN={
"finance/0":"Think of income as water flowing into a bucket. Your salary is the tap, and the size of the tap is gross pay, the number printed on your offer letter. But before the water reaches your bucket, taxes and deductions drink some on the way, so what lands is your take-home pay. That is the number you actually live on. Now picture three taps: a job, a side gig and an investment that pays you back. One tap can stop suddenly (layoffs happen), but three keep the bucket filling. A good first goal is not a bigger tap but a second one. If you earn ₹30,000 take-home and a weekend gig adds ₹6,000, you just gave yourself a 20% raise without asking anyone.",
"finance/1":"A budget is a plan that tells every rupee where to go before it wanders off. Imagine your salary arrives as 10 coins. The 50/30/20 rule says: put 5 coins in the NEEDS box (rent, food, transport), 3 in WANTS (movies, snacks, shopping) and 2 in the FUTURE box (savings and investing). When a box is empty, you stop spending from it. That is all budgeting is. It feels restrictive only when you do it by guessing, because then you find the money is gone on the 20th. With 40,000 take-home, that is 20,000 for needs, 12,000 for wants and 8,000 for the future, which is 96,000 saved in a year without feeling poor.",
"finance/8":"Compound interest is a snowball rolling downhill. A snowball starts small, and every turn it picks up snow, but here is the trick: the new snow also sticks and picks up more snow on the next turn. Money works the same way. You put in 1,000 and earn 8%, so you have 1,080. Next year you earn 8% on 1,080, not on 1,000, so you gain 86.4 instead of 80. The difference looks tiny, but after 30 years that snowball is 10,063 while a simple, non-snowball 8% would give only 3,400. Time is the hill. Starting at 22 instead of 32 can double your final amount, even if you save the same each month. A quick shortcut is the rule of 72: divide 72 by the rate and you get the years to double.",
"computers/0":"Programming is writing a recipe so precise that even a robot with zero common sense can cook it. If you tell a person 'make tea', they fill in the gaps. A computer needs every step: boil water, put a tea bag in a cup, pour the water, wait three minutes, remove the bag. Those steps are your code. A loop is 'repeat until the cup is full', and an if-statement is 'if the guest wants sugar, add a spoon'. Variables are labelled jars that hold values, like sugar = 2. Languages such as Python just let you write the recipe in neat, nearly English words. When something breaks, that is a bug, usually one missing step in the recipe, and fixing it is half the fun.",
"computers/4":"The internet is a gigantic postal system where every letter is torn into small postcards called packets. Imagine you order a pizza from another city. Your request is cut into postcards, each with the sender's address, the receiver's address and a number like 3 of 8. They travel through many post offices (routers), each choosing the fastest road at that moment, and they may even arrive out of order. The pizza shop's computer sorts them by number and reads the whole order. DNS is the phone book that turns a name like google.com into an address, and HTTPS is a sealed, signed envelope so no one reads your card on the way. All of it takes a fraction of a second.",
"computers/8":"Machine learning is teaching by examples instead of rules. To teach a child what a cat is, you do not recite 'pointy ears, whiskers, four legs'. You show a hundred photos and say cat or not cat, and the child's brain finds the pattern. A neural network does the same with numbers. Each photo goes in as pixels, passes through layers of tiny dials, and comes out as a guess such as cat 70%. If the answer is wrong, the dials are nudged a little, and this repeats millions of times until the guesses are mostly right. That nudging step is called training. The scary secret is that it is only pattern matching on a huge scale, which is why the quality of the examples matters more than anything else.",
"chemistry/0.0":"An atom is a tiny solar system, only the planets are fuzzy clouds. The sun in the middle is the nucleus, packed with protons (positive) and neutrons (no charge). Whizzing around it are electrons (negative), which are about 1,800 times lighter. The number of protons is the atom's name tag: 1 proton is hydrogen, 6 is carbon, 8 is oxygen, 79 is gold. Change one proton and you get a different element completely. The electrons sit in shells like seats in a stadium, the inner row holds 2, the next 8. The atom is mostly empty space: if the nucleus were a marble in the middle of a football stadium, the electrons would be specks near the stands. The outermost electrons decide how an atom makes friends, which is chemistry.",
"chemistry/0.3":"Matter is like a crowd at a concert. In a solid, the crowd is packed and everybody only shakes in place, so it keeps its shape, like an ice cube. Warm things up and people start to move around each other: that is a liquid, which flows and takes the shape of its container, like water. Heat even more and everybody runs wildly in all directions with huge gaps between them: that is a gas, which fills every corner of the room. Temperature is just how fast the crowd is moving. Melting, boiling and freezing are the moments the crowd changes mood. There is a fourth state too, plasma, which is a gas so hot that electrons are torn off, and that is what lightning and the Sun are made of."
};
